    Walk infers a generic dotted method's own static arguments, alone or among its receiver's overloads, and chooses it on its declared domain

    Climb batch 17, rung W (wip/rung-walk-method-sargs, e42e05498..ca1a6896e), composed from the branch's net change with the skeptic's expected failures for rows 765 and 766. The team's walk applied a generic method's symbolic instance when a dotted call wrote no static argument, so such a call failed, and an overloaded method set never chose the generic method, as the team's library says of distribute (Generator2.fss:81). The specification infers a method's static arguments from the context of the invocation and chooses a declaration on the parameter types it declares (inference.tex:47-75); rung L's generic distribute needs it under walk.

    Folded from the rung's record: FACTS F-253 added, F-26 and F-248 rewritten, and F-252 (rung G's) made true of a generic dotted method at a call; ledger rows 760 to 766 (NEW-W-1 to NEW-W-7) added, their numbers put into inference.tex's two walk boxes, Appendix I's entry "The inference of a call's static arguments" and the test XXXDispatchGenericMethodBesidePlain. The notes on rows 510, 364, 723, 307, 744 and 567 were refused (full or closed) and are listed in the batch's RECORD.md. Row 21 closes after the rungs.

